{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgTD46W4brDUDT4fBHvpR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the reference query plan (status: ready, 10 queries) to `reference_query_plan.json` and `reference_query_plan.md`. No references were retrieved.\n\n**What the plan is based on**\n- **Final model:** the selected program is the plain log-linear ridge regression on graph statistics (`ridge_log_linear_v0`). Its average error on the log scale is 0.183, identical across 3 seeds. The other model in memory, the physics-scaling mixture, was implemented but never evaluated, so I left it out of the plan.\n- **Comparators** (taken from the evaluator code in `task/evaluate.py`), as average log-scale error:\n\n| Comparator | Log error |\n|---|---|\n| 2m(n−1) bound (Aleliunas et al.) | 1.84 |\n| (4/27)n³ bound (Feige) | 1.60 |\n| Better of those two bounds | 1.40 |\n| Matthews bound (max hitting time × harmonic number) | 0.61 |\n| OLS reference | 0.53 |\n| Constant predictor | 0.94 |\n\n- **Test families:** path, cycle, star, complete, 2D grid, binary tree, lollipop, random tree, Erdős–Rényi, random 3-regular.\n- **Per-family results:** the model beats the best bound by the most on complete, Erdős–Rényi and random 3-regular graphs. It gains least on the lollipop, where the confidence interval reaches zero. Its own error is highest on grid, complete and Erdős–Rényi graphs.\n\n**The 10 queries**\n1. The paper behind the 2m(n−1) bound (Aleliunas et al.).\n2. Feige's tight upper bound.\n3. Matthews' bound.\n4. Lovász's survey of random walks on graphs.\n5. The resistance/commute-time characterisation of cover time, which explains why edge count, degree and diameter should be predictive.\n6. Feige's n log n lower bound, which explains why the bounds are loosest on dense and well-connected graphs.\n7. Cooper–Frieze cover-time results for random regular and random graphs.\n8. Cover time on 2D grids and tori.\n9. Maximum